This is a ROS based open source mapping project named maplab. 
When generating, analyzing, or modifying code related to the Maplab framework, strictly adhere to its fundamental two-stage, modular architecture. Differentiate all operations based on this structure:

Front-End Focus (Real-time/On-Session Processing):

Prioritize code for robust, independent visual-inertial data acquisition and initial VIMap (Visual-Inertial Map) generation.

Recognize and correctly interact with front-end nodes like ROVIOLI (or similar custom implementations) responsible for on-robot/on-session sensor data processing and creating foundational map segments.

Ensure generated code for this stage is optimized for efficiency and real-time constraints where applicable.

Backend Focus (Offline Processing, Optimization, and Research):

Prioritize code for comprehensive offline map manipulation using functionalities akin to the Maplab console and server.

This includes operations such as: detailed map optimization, multi-session/multi-robot map merging, global loop closure detection and enforcement, data analysis, visualization, and batch processing for algorithmic evaluation.

When dealing with multi-robot scenarios, focus on code that facilitates consistent global map creation and maintenance via server components.

Modularity and Extensibility (Research Platform):

Leverage Maplab's inherent modularity when tasked with integrating new algorithms (e.g., for localization, mapping, place recognition, or VIO).

Ensure new code modules integrate cleanly with existing Maplab APIs, data structures (especially the VIMap), and communication protocols.

Propose extensions or modifications that respect and enhance the framework's research-oriented, plug-and-play nature.

Overall, all code interactions must reflect an understanding of Maplab as a platform designed for both practical SLAM applications and advanced research, emphasizing the separation between online map creation and offline refinement/analysis.